---
import { scoring } from "../lib/scoring";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { loadSponsors } from "../lib/sponsors";
import { latestDay, loadTaxonomy } from "../lib/data";
import { fieldLabel, publicEditions, type Field } from "../lib/editions";

const { weights, top_n, category_floor, max_per_category, history_days_for_own_baseline, rise_k } = scoring;
const day = latestDay("computational");
const { contact } = loadSponsors();
const fields: Field[] = ["computational", ...publicEditions];
---

<Base description="How TrendBio selects, groups and ranks research across scientific fields." canonicalPath="/about/" title="About — TrendBio" lastRun={day?.run.finished}>
  <article class="prose" aria-labelledby="about-heading">
    <h1 id="about-heading">About TrendBio</h1>
    <p>TrendBio groups papers by the research question they address and ranks the topics within each field. The <a href="/fields/">science directory</a> lists the daily editions and dated pilots, including <a href="/computer-information/">Computer Science</a>. Each ranking links to the papers behind it.</p>
    <p><a href="/biology/">All Biology (beta)</a> groups experimental, observational and computational studies from selected bioRxiv subjects and PubMed journals. It builds its own rankings. The <a href="/">CompBio edition</a> keeps its specialist rankings and full archive.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/physics/">Physics edition</a> ranks arXiv preprints separately and links the papers behind each day’s topics.</p>
    <p>A paper’s main result determines where it belongs. A general learning algorithm fits Computer Science, a new tool for genome analysis fits CompBio, and a biological discovery made with that tool fits All Biology. Papers with contributions to more than one field can appear in multiple editions.</p>
    <h2>Computational biology sources</h2>
    <p>We collect preprints from bioRxiv and arXiv, published papers from PubMed, code and tool activity from GitHub, and announcements from industry feeds. Each topic links to its underlying evidence, with preprints linked to their published versions when known.</p>
    <h2>How ranking works</h2>
    <p>Four signals contribute to a topic’s score.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Growth ({weights.rise * 100}%) compares the topic’s current paper count with a baseline. The ratio adds {rise_k} to both counts so topics with few papers can be compared.</li>
      <li>Evidence diversity ({weights.diversity * 100}%) rewards a mix of evidence types.</li>
      <li>Code activity ({weights.code_velocity * 100}%) measures gains in GitHub stars.</li>
      <li>Volume ({weights.volume * 100}%) reflects the number of papers.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In a seven-day edition, topics with less than {history_days_for_own_baseline} days of history use the median paper count across topics scored in that field on that day. Older topics use the average of the counts one, two, three and four weeks earlier. A missing week counts as zero.</p>
    <p>Longer-window pilots use the average of up to four earlier, non-overlapping windows of the same length when at least two have papers. Otherwise they use the field median.</p>
    <p>A small penalty applies to topics ranked the day before. Daily topics with identical scores are ordered alphabetically by topic ID. Their cards show the range of tied positions.</p>
    <p>Code and industry items attach to at most one research topic when their similarity clears a calibrated threshold. They contribute evidence, diversity and code velocity; paper counts, rise and topic continuity stay paper-only. The separate “From industry” list shows up to eight relevant announcements first seen in the preceding seven days, whether or not they attach to a topic.</p>
    <p>Code appears in the “From code” list because repositories rarely join research topics by text similarity.</p>
    <p>“Shared by researchers” covers Bluesky only and never affects rankings or scores. It excludes accounts linking 20 or more papers a week and can include authors promoting their own work.</p>
    <p>Daily, weekly and monthly rankings select up to {top_n} topics. Selection reserves {category_floor} place per represented category while space is available, then fills the remaining places by score, with at most {max_per_category} per category.</p>
    <p>In CompBio, Biology and Physics, weekly rankings run Monday through Sunday and monthly rankings use calendar months. Both add up all daily topic scores in that period, including days a topic missed the daily ranking. Ties go to more days ranked, then more unique items, then alphabetical topic ID.</p>
    <p>Each entry shows days ranked, best rank, unique items and peak growth. Its headline comes from the last daily edition that ranked it during the period. Topics without a published headline in that period use their automatic label. “So far” marks a period still in progress.</p>
    <p>Tool names under Top 20 headlines are copied from paper titles and do not affect ranking.</p>
    <h2>Machine-generated summaries</h2>
    <p>Headlines, summaries and syntheses are machine-generated and may contain errors. Follow the evidence links and consult the original sources before relying on a claim.</p>
    <h2>Feeds</h2>
    <p>Subscribe to the daily Top 5 for your field.</p>
    <ul>{fields.map(field => {
      const base = field === "computational" ? "" : `/${field}`;
      return <li><a href={`${base}/feed.xml`}>{fieldLabel(field)}</a>
        <details><summary>{fieldLabel(field)} category feeds</summary>
          {loadTaxonomy(field).map((category, index) => <>{index > 0 && " · "}<a href={`${base}/feed/${category.slug}.xml`}>{category.label}</a></>)}
        </details></li>;
    })}</ul>
    <p>The site uses Cloudflare Web Analytics, which sets no cookies, to count page views. Cloudflare may set a security cookie for bot protection. Search terms appear in the results URL and your browser history.</p>
    {contact && <>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p><!--email_off--><a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a><!--/email_off--></p>
    </>}
  </article>
</Base>
